WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.241
Look at the colour code of a web page.

00:00:03.241 --> 00:00:08.039
Something like this: a hash sign, then six characters, 0F6E73.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.980
That's the teal you see in this video.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:16.210
Why do programmers write colours, and memory, in hexadecimal?

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.454
Because hex is a compact way to write binary.

00:00:20.454 --> 00:00:23.491
Every hex digit is exactly four bits.

00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:24.640
Let's see why.

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:29.033
This is Lecture One, part four, the last part.

00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:33.841
We'll convert between binary, octal and hex by grouping digits.

00:00:33.841 --> 00:00:38.583
Then we'll meet the units of data, and see how memory is organised.

00:00:39.366 --> 00:00:41.236
8 is 2 to the power of 3.

00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:45.424
So each octal digit matches exactly three binary digits.

00:00:45.424 --> 00:00:50.659
And each group of three binary digits matches exactly one octal digit.

00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:53.126
It's a one-to-one correspondence.

00:00:53.476 --> 00:00:55.000
0 is 000.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.524
1 is 001.

00:00:56.524 --> 00:00:58.048
2 is 010.

00:00:58.048 --> 00:01:03.636
And so on, up to 7, which is 111.

00:01:03.886 --> 00:01:13.728
To convert octal to binary, replace each digit with its three bits, and keep the order.

00:01:13.728 --> 00:01:18.366
0.754, in octal, becomes 0.111, 101, 100.

00:01:18.716 --> 00:01:23.916
The zeros at the front of the integer part, and at the end of the fraction, can be dropped.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:33.489
16 is 2 to the power of 4, so each hex digit matches exactly four bits.

00:01:33.489 --> 00:01:34.727
9 is 1001.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.327
The letter A is 1010.

00:01:37.327 --> 00:01:39.060
And F is 1111.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.921
Take 4C.2E, in hex.

00:01:43.921 --> 00:01:46.296
4 is 0100.

00:01:46.296 --> 00:01:48.671
C is 1100.

00:01:48.671 --> 00:01:51.045
2 is 0010.

00:01:51.045 --> 00:01:54.370
And E is 1110.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:03.760
Drop the zeros on the outside, and you get 1001100.0010111.

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:08.773
Going back, cut the binary number into groups, starting from the point.

00:02:09.123 --> 00:02:13.232
In the integer part, start at the point and go left.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:17.103
In the fraction, start at the point and go right.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:21.923
If the last group is short, pad it with zeros on the outside.

00:02:22.523 --> 00:02:27.284
Take 11101.01, and convert it to octal.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:31.191
From the point, going left: 101.

00:02:31.191 --> 00:02:36.563
Then a short group, 11, which we pad to 011.

00:02:36.913 --> 00:02:41.913
Going right: 01, padded to 010.

00:02:42.263 --> 00:02:47.583
Replace each group with its octal digit, and you get 35.2.

00:02:48.366 --> 00:02:51.611
Pause the video and predict.

00:02:51.611 --> 00:02:57.406
What is the same number, 11101.01, in hexadecimal?

00:02:57.956 --> 00:03:02.814
Make groups of four from the point.

00:03:02.814 --> 00:03:09.755
Going left: 1101, then a single 1, padded to 0001.

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:14.196
Going right: 01, padded to 0100.

00:03:14.546 --> 00:03:18.866
So the answer is 1D.4, in hex.

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:21.868
Pad only on the outside.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.662
0.01, in binary, is a quarter.

00:03:24.662 --> 00:03:27.083
But 0.0001 is a sixteenth.

00:03:27.083 --> 00:03:31.273
Padding on the wrong side changes the number.

00:03:31.623 --> 00:03:33.977
Python agrees with our answers.

00:03:33.977 --> 00:03:37.469
The `hex` function turns binary 11101 into 1D.

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:42.783
And to decimal gives the same value for both versions of the fraction.

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:46.103
And here's why programmers like hex.

00:03:46.103 --> 00:03:50.146
A byte, 8 bits, is always exactly two hex digits.

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:51.713
Eight ones make FF.

00:03:51.713 --> 00:03:55.013
And our teal colour is just three bytes.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.798
Now, the units of data.

00:03:57.798 --> 00:04:02.140
A bit, short for binary digit, is a single 0 or 1.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.440
It's the smallest unit of information.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.446
A byte is 8 bits.

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:11.927
Each extra bit doubles the number of patterns.

00:04:11.927 --> 00:04:17.673
So one byte has 2 to the power of 8, which is 256 patterns.

00:04:17.673 --> 00:04:20.790
The whole numbers from 0 to 255.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:24.492
Bigger amounts use bigger units.

00:04:24.492 --> 00:04:29.414
A kilobyte is 2 to the power of 10 bytes: 1024.

00:04:29.414 --> 00:04:34.860
A megabyte is 1024 kilobytes, about a million bytes.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:41.050
A gigabyte is about a billion bytes, and a terabyte is about a trillion.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.848
Strictly, 2 to the power of 10 bytes is called a kibibyte.

00:04:45.848 --> 00:04:48.839
Disk makers use powers of 1000 instead.

00:04:48.839 --> 00:04:53.363
That's why a 1 terabyte drive, 10 to the power of 12 bytes,

00:04:53.363 --> 00:04:57.120
shows up on your computer as about 931 gigabytes.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:03.830
In this course, a kilobyte means 1024 bytes, unless we say otherwise.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.832
Text is stored as numbers too.

00:05:06.832 --> 00:05:08.765
Each character has a code.

00:05:08.765 --> 00:05:14.120
In UTF-8, the most common encoding, every English letter takes one byte.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.989
The word 'Crew' is 4 bytes:

00:05:18.989 --> 00:05:22.670
67, 114, 101, and 119.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:28.346
The capital letter 'C' is the number 67,

00:05:28.346 --> 00:05:31.940
which is the byte 01000011.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.751
But not every character is one byte.

00:05:34.751 --> 00:05:37.964
A Chinese character takes three bytes in UTF-8.

00:05:37.964 --> 00:05:43.090
So these four characters, meaning 'artificial intelligence', take 12 bytes.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.886
A Python string counts characters.

00:05:46.886 --> 00:05:49.520
Its encoding counts bytes.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.647
Finally, memory.

00:05:51.647 --> 00:05:55.351
Main memory is an ordered sequence of bytes.

00:05:55.351 --> 00:05:59.055
Every byte has a unique number, its address.

00:05:59.055 --> 00:06:03.180
To read or write data, the CPU names the address.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.494
Here's part of a memory.

00:06:05.494 --> 00:06:09.748
It holds the text 'Crew', at addresses 2000 to 2003,

00:06:09.748 --> 00:06:12.530
and the number 3, at address 2004.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.502
Notice that the bytes don't say what they mean.

00:06:16.502 --> 00:06:21.202
This byte is the letter 'C' if the program treats it as text,

00:06:21.202 --> 00:06:23.899
and 67 if it treats it as a number.

00:06:23.899 --> 00:06:25.440
The program decides.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.219
In assembly or C, the programmer works with addresses directly.

00:06:30.219 --> 00:06:32.673
In Python, you give your data a name,

00:06:32.673 --> 00:06:36.520
and Python and the operating system decide where it lives.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.891
That's the key difference between low-level and high-level languages.

00:06:40.891 --> 00:06:46.310
In Lecture Two, you'll see that a Python name refers to an object stored somewhere in memory.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.460
Let's sum up.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.618
Binary and octal: groups of three bits.

00:06:51.618 --> 00:06:53.850
Binary and hex: groups of four.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:58.890
Group outwards from the point, and pad with zeros only on the outside.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.964
A bit is one binary digit, and a byte is 8 bits.

00:07:03.964 --> 00:07:10.360
Kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes and terabytes each go up by 1024.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.350
Memory is a sequence of bytes, each with an address,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.430
and the program decides what the bytes mean.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:19.887
Here's one to try.

00:07:19.887 --> 00:07:25.506
Convert 111110 from binary to octal, and to hex.

00:07:25.506 --> 00:07:28.900
Both answers should equal 62.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.461
That's the end of Lecture One.

00:07:31.461 --> 00:07:37.090
In Lecture Two, we'll start writing Python: data types, operators, input and output.

